 <iati-activity default-currency="USD" hierarchy="1" iati-activities:version="2.03" xml:lang="EN" xmlns:iati-activities="http://d-portal.org/xmlns/iati-activities">
  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-BLZ201</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved institutional policy or regulatory framework to strengthen governance, financial management or sustainability for the delivery of gender equitable social protection</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Belize&apos;s social protection system operates in a fragmented structure, failing to leverage existing resources and technical capacities across stakeholders. This has exacerbated the vulnerabilities of the urban poor, people with disabilities, and rural populations, especially in the context of COVID-19. The ILO has attempted to address these issues by supporting the preparation and validation of the National Social Protection Strategy, building on recommendations from the Actuarial Review and Unemployment Insurance Cost Assessment, which resulted in the adoption of an unemployment insurance scheme. Key activities includde revising the self-employed scheme, enhancing social protection statistics, and introducing changes to social security benefits.

Beneficiaries of this initiative include vulnerable groups such as the urban poor, rural populations, and MSMEs, who will benefit from improved social protection and economic opportunities. The work will be done in collaboration with the Ministry of Labour, the Statistical Institute of Belize, and external consultants. The ILO will provide technical assistance, expert advice, advocacy, training, and facilitation, ensuring gender equality principles are considered. This strategy aims to create a more comprehensive and coordinated social protection system in Belize.</narrative>
  </description>
